1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,399
A record high in October 2007. Then the warnings piled up.

2
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:11,422
An index falls 0.2% or more, on heavier volume than yesterday.

3
00:00:11,423 --> 00:00:14,932
Old-school read: big funds are quietly selling.

4
00:00:14,933 --> 00:00:19,001
Count them over the last 25 trading days.

5
00:00:19,002 --> 00:00:23,466
O'Neil: four or five in a month spells trouble.

6
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:27,551
Here's the S&P 500 in 2007. Watch the count.

7
00:00:27,552 --> 00:00:31,999
On October 9 it closed at a record. Count: one.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,266
Eight trading days later, the count was five.

9
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:40,532
By November 7 it hit eight. Prices kept slipping.

10
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:45,214
By March 2008 the S&P 500 was down 19%.

11
00:00:45,215 --> 00:00:49,066
A year later it was down 57%.

12
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:52,739
It's a warning, not a crystal ball.

13
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:57,599
When the count piles up, go easy on new buys.

14
00:00:59,933 --> 00:01:03,949
Lore 013. Next: Lore 014, Replay: would you buy here?
